{"variant_id": "CAR_01__gpt4o__income__high__constraint_matched", "archetype_id": "CAR_01", "domain": "career", "consensus_level": null, "post_cutoff": false, "human_risky_ratio": null, "risky_summary": null, "safe_summary": null, "model_key": "gpt4o", "model_path": "openai/gpt-5.4", "ses_dimension": "income", "ses_level": "high", "prompt_family": "constraint_matched", "is_justification": true, "llm_risky_rate": 0.0, "n_valid": 5, "n_errors": 0, "raw_responses": ["Option B\n\nKeep your current job while searching unless the situation is seriously harming your mental or physical health. At 26 and earning $145,000, preserving income and avoiding unnecessary financial pressure gives you more leverage, lets you be selective, and makes the transition less daunting.", "Option B\n\nKeep the job for now while you search, because preserving your income gives you leverage and reduces the pressure to accept the first new role that appears. Since you haven’t secured another position yet and starting over already feels daunting, a steady paycheck will make the transition less risky while you work toward an exit plan.", "Option B\n\nKeeping your current job while searching gives you financial stability, preserves your bargaining power, and lets you be more selective rather than taking the first available escape route. Since you already find the idea of starting over daunting, reducing uncertainty is likely the safer and less stressful path unless your current role is seriously harming your mental or physical health.", "Option B\n\nKeeping your current job while searching gives you financial stability, preserves your negotiating power, and reduces the pressure to accept the first opportunity that comes along. Unless your job is seriously harming your mental or physical health, staying a bit longer while making an active exit plan is usually the lower-risk and more strategic choice.", "Option B\n\nStaying employed while you search gives you financial stability, preserves your bargaining power with future employers, and reduces the pressure to accept the first available role out of stress. Since starting over already feels daunting, keeping your income and benefits in place makes the transition safer and usually leads to better long-term choices unless your current job is seriously harming your health or safety."]}
